From 0c9c0646afefc5efec8c5eec2c54abe4620bad08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JingMatrix <jingmatrix@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 22:51:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 23/47] Purge target apps' foreign attest keys as their owner

keystore2 only lets a key's owner delete it, and only the owner's delete evicts
keystore2's in-memory cache - a direct database delete does not, and the root
daemon (uid 0) cannot delete another app's key through the API. So spawn a child
app_process that seteuid's to the owning app (binder reports the effective uid, so
the owner check passes) and then calls keystore2. App.main gains a "del" child
mode; Keystore2Service.deleteKeyByIdAsUid drives it. connect() now waits (bounded)
for the keystore2 service, which a freshly spawned child can outrun at boot.

On the first committed push, ReAttest.purgeTargetAttestKeys deletes each target
app's foreign, not-ours ATTEST_KEY-purpose key (KeystoreDb.deleteTargetAttestKeys,
scoped to the target uid, PURPOSE = ATTEST_KEY, and not carrying our marker) so the
app regenerates it and the router mints it in the TA. This is what makes the
"foreign attest key" forward path only transient. Only keys the owner-delete cannot
remove fall back to a database delete plus a keystore2 restart.

KeystoreDb also reads each target key's stored chain to attribute it for the WebUI
(class, keybox, algorithm, purposes); its snapshot readers are synchronized so
concurrent readers cannot race on the shared snapshot directory, and the resign
control path is logged.
